#let lesson = (
title: [Trial Introductory Flight]

,aim: [
Introduce a prospective student to the experience of powered flight training; demonstrate the primary effects of the flight controls; and allow the instructor to make informal observations of the student's suitability for continued training.
]

,overview: [
The purpose of The Trial Introductory Flight (TIF) is threefold: to give the prospective student a genuine, enjoyable experience of flying; to satisfy the pre-commencement requirements of the RAAus FOM Section 2.06; and to allow the instructor to make informal observations relevant to suitability for training.

The TIF is not an attempt to deliver the Effects of Controls lesson. The tone throughout should be relaxed, encouraging, and enjoyable. The student arrived curious; they should leave enthusiastic. The instructor's observations are made quietly, in the background, without making the flight feel like a test.

*Note on regulatory requirements before the TIF commences:* Under RAAus FOM Section 2.06, before any flying training may be conducted (including a TIF), the student must: (a) be advised of the risks associated with recreational flying; (b) have at least temporary RAAus membership; (c) be assessed by the instructor as having sufficient English language and cognitive skills; and (d) if under 18, have written parental or guardian consent. These requirements are addressed in the ground brief below.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
*Note: The TIF does not have formal learning outcomes assessed against a progression standard. The following describes what the student will experience and what the instructor will observe.*

#text(size:12pt)[*What the student will experience*]
- The general environment of a flight training school: aircraft, equipment
- A pre-flight inspection: what is checked and why
- The cockpit: controls, instruments, harness, door, intercom
- The sounds, motion, and *visual references* of flight
- The primary effect of the elevator and aileron
- The effect of positive pitch stability: the aircraft returning toward the original attitude after a displacement
- Gentle straight and level flight: maintaining a constant attitude using the horizon
- What flight training at GCSFT involves: syllabus structure, cost, time, solo, certificate

#text(size:12pt)[*What the instructor will observe — informally throughout*]
- *English language and cognitive skills*: Can the student understand verbal instructions clearly? Can they read and acknowledge written material? Can they follow a simple in-flight control instruction? (RAAus FOM S2.06(c) — this is a regulatory requirement before the flight may proceed)
- *Ability to follow instruction*: When asked to apply a simple control input, does the student respond correctly? Are they able to retain and apply a basic verbal instruction in the flight environment?
- *Reaction to the flight environment*: Confidence, anxiety, curiosity, sensory overload — does the student appear engaged and stable, or overwhelmed?
- *Motion sickness*: Any signs of discomfort; history disclosed; early warning signs noted
- *Apparent physical fitness*: No formal medical assessment is conducted, but obvious impairments to safe training should be noted
- *Interest in continuing*: Does the student want to enrol after the flight?
]

,theory_knowledge: [
No formal theory requirements for the TIF. The following references are relevant if the student wishes to read ahead before Lesson 0:
- Bob Tait's RAAus Study Guide: Pg 101–107 (primary controls, further effects)
- Bob Tait's RAAus Study Guide: Pg 3 (IMSAFE)
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 30 minutes \
Equipment required: GCSFT Training Syllabus overview (printed or digital), RAAus membership form, Section 3.02 risk notification document, aircraft model \

*Welcome and Introduction* (3 minutes)
- Welcome the student; introduce GCSFT and the instructors
- Brief overview of what today involves: a ground brief, a pre-flight inspection, and a flight

*RAAus FOM Section 2.06 — Regulatory Pre-Commencement Requirements* (5 minutes)

_These items are mandatory before the flight may proceed:_

- *Risk notification (FOM S3.02)*: The student is advised that recreational flying carries inherent risk; collisions, mechanical failures, and weather events can and do cause incidents and fatalities; the risks are managed but not eliminated; the student acknowledges this verbally or in writing
- *Temporary RAAus membership*: Temporary membership is generally arranged before flight however this must be confirmed by the instructor; a student may fly for up to 3 hours dual instruction before full membership is required, but temporary membership must be in place before the flight
- *English language and cognitive skills assessment (FOM S2.06(c))*: The instructor confirms, in their opinion, that the student has sufficient English and cognitive skills for the flight; this is assessed through the ground brief conversation; if there is genuine doubt, the flight does not proceed
- *Under-18 requirement*: If the student is under 18, written consent from a parent or guardian must be sighted before the flight; this must have been arranged in advance; without it, the flight does not proceed
- *Health overview (FOM S2.06(e))*: Brief overview of: health standards (driver's licence medical); not flying when ill or injured; drug and alcohol restrictions; caution on medications; students are expected to self-assess using IMSAFE before every flight

*IMSAFE Introduction* (3 minutes)
- Introduce IMSAFE: Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue, Emotion
- Explain this is applied before every flight without exception; today, the student confirms they are fit to participate

*Motion Sickness* (3 minutes)
- Explain: motion sickness is common in the first few flights; it is not a barrier to training; most students adapt within a few lessons
- If symptoms develop in flight: inform the instructor immediately; do not try to push through it; there is an air sickness bag in the cockpit
- Early warning signs to communicate to instructor: nausea, excessive sweating, pale appearance, feeling unusually warm

*Overview of Flight Training at GCSFT* (5 minutes)
- Training structure: Phase 1 (ab initio), Phase 2 (circuits and solo), Phase 3 (training area), Phase 4 (flight test)
- Typical hours: minimum 20 hours total aeronautical experience including 5 hours PIC; most students take 30–35 hours
- Approximate cost: provide current pricing guide
- First solo: typically around hours
- Theory exams: 5 exams; self-study with paid assistance available
- Time commitment: typically 1–2 lessons per week to maintain continuity is ideal, but we suggest not less than once per month; gaps slow progress significantly
- The RPC: privileges and limitations

*Control Handover* (3 minutes)
- All control handovers to be formally acknowledged with "your controls"/"my controls"/"follow-me-through"
- Confirm student understands and can apply the procedure

*Aircraft Familiarisation* (3 minutes)
- Walk to the aircraft: instructor demonstrates the pre-flight inspection at a high level; explain the purpose of each major check area
- Identify the access door, door latch, and release mechanism; student practises opening and closing

*Cockpit Orientation* (3 minutes)
- Seat and harness: both adjusted; make sure the student is comfortable and can reach all the controls
- Primary controls: stick/column, rudder pedals
- Intercom: how to talk; PTT button (if applicable); confirm student can communicate with instructor
- Hatch: confirm student knows how to open it from the inside; in an emergency, the instructor will direct them; do not open in flight
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor*]
- Confirm all FOM S2.06 pre-commencement requirements are met before proceeding
- If under 18: confirm written parental/guardian consent is present and signed
- Review maintenance release independently; sign the release
- Check NOTAMs; check NAIPS TAF; confirm conditions suitable for the TIF
- Complete operations whiteboard
- Assess the student's English comprehension and responsiveness during the ground brief; if genuine doubt exists, do not proceed

#text(size:12pt)[*Student*]
- Acknowledge the risk notification (verbal or written, per GCSFT procedure)
- Complete IMSAFE self-assessment with instructor guidance; confirm fitness to participate today
- Complete temporary RAAus membership form if not already a member
- Provide parental/guardian consent documentation if under 18
]

,in_flight_tasks:[

#text(size:12pt)[*Control Handover Procedure — Establish Before Transfer*]
Before any control is transferred to the student, establish the handover procedure clearly
- Confirm student understanding before the first transfer

#text(size:12pt)[*Departure and Transit — Instructor*]
- Instructor manages all startup, taxi, radio calls, and takeoff; student observes
- Student may rest hands and feet lightly on the controls to feel the inputs — follow-through only; the student does not add any input
- During the transit to the training area point out positive stability in pitch - the fact that the aircraft generally returns to straight and level

#text(size:12pt)[*Demonstration and Student Experience*]
Each demonstration follows the same pattern: instructor demonstrates with brief patter; transfers control to student; student applies the same input; instructor transfers back.

_The horizon as the primary reference_
- Before any control demonstration: point out the horizon as the primary visual reference. Point out the cruise (straight and level) attitude, and that we always come back to this "home" position.

_Primary Controls_
- Instructor: brief demonstration (student follows through) of the primary effects of the elevator and the ailerons. Student follows through and observes the effects on the aircraft, and the visual reference.
- Student: controls the aircraft's pitch and roll to maintain straight and level flight and gentle turns.

_Straight and Level_
- Instructor: patters the visual reference and guides the student in maintaining straight and level flight.

_Turning, Climbing and Descending_
- Instructor: patters the control movements (with the student in control) for a gentle turn; patters student through a gentle climb and descent by adjusting the throttle and having the student adjust and maintain the correct pitch; instructor can trim the aircraft and handle the rudder as needed.
- Student: follows instruction to change attitude; holds attitude in correct position using smooth control input. 

#text(size:12pt)[*Return and Landing — Instructor*]
- Instructor takes control before any circuit join; patters what is happening at each stage
- Student may follow through on the controls at instructor's discretion

#text(size:12pt)[*Throughout the flight — instructor observes:*]
- Response to verbal instructions in the air: does the student follow simple control inputs correctly?
- Confidence and composure: calm? Anxious but managing? Overwhelmed?
- Signs of motion sickness: note any pallor, sweating, or discomfort; terminate the training area exercises and return early if symptoms appear
- Language: quality of communication through the intercom; any comprehension gaps?
- Engagement: is the student asking questions? Curious? Or withdrawn and passive?
]

,common_errors:[
*There are no errors to correct in a TIF in the usual sense. The following describes situations requiring instructor response:*

- *Overcontrolling*: Very common; correct with calm voice only — "Lighter touch — just two fingers. The aircraft is sensitive." Do not take control unless a genuine safety issue develops.
- *Signs of motion sickness developing*: Do not wait for symptoms to become severe; return immediately and discontinue training area exercises; explain that this is very common and almost always resolves after a few lessons.
- *Student appears overwhelmed or anxious*: Reduce the workload; stop the exercises; fly straight and level; enjoy the view; do not push; the experience can still be positive without all the planned exercises.
- *Comprehension gaps identified during flight*: Note these; if instructions are genuinely not understood in the air, take control and hold; debrief on the ground.
]

,post_flight:[
- *Student Experience Debrief* (5 min): "What did you enjoy? What surprised you? How did it feel when you had the controls?"
- *Instructor Informal Assessment — recorded privately in the lesson record* (not shared with student verbatim):
  - *English and cognitive skills*: Adequate / Requires further observation / Insufficient for training
  - *Ability to follow instruction*: Followed simple verbal instructions correctly / Required multiple repetitions / Could not follow
  - *Composure in the flight environment*: Engaged and relaxed / Anxious but managed / Overwhelmed
  - *Motion sickness*: None evident / Minor and manageable / Significant — monitor in early lessons
  - *Interest in continuing*: Wants to enrol / Undecided / Not interested
  - *Overall recommendation*: Recommend training / Further observation recommended / Not recommended — specific reason noted
- *Enrolment Discussion* (if student is interested) (10 min):
  - Walk through the training pathway, approximate cost and time
  - Describe Lesson 0 and what it covers
  - Provide the GCSFT student information package
  - Next steps: complete RAAus membership, obtain Bob Tait textbook, book Lesson 0
  - Note: the student must have full RAAus membership before exceeding 3 hours of dual instruction
- *If the student is not interested*: Thank them sincerely; provide the information package anyway; offer to answer any questions they develop later
]

,standards_for_progression:[
*The TIF does not use the P/S/C/NC grading system. The instructor records an informal suitability assessment using the following categories.*

#text(size:12pt)[*Recommend for training*]
The student demonstrates the following during the TIF:
- English comprehension sufficient to follow simple verbal instructions in the air without confusion
- Reasonable composure in the flight environment (anxiety is normal and expected; it is not a barrier)
- Ability to follow at least one simple control instruction correctly when given in the air
- No impairment (obvious physical, cognitive, or substance-related) that would create unacceptable risk in early training
- No obvious barrier to the FOM S2.06 pre-commencement requirements being met before Lesson 0

#text(size:12pt)[*Further observation recommended*]
The instructor has a concern that may resolve with more information:
- English comprehension was borderline during the ground brief or in flight; a further ground assessment before Lesson 0 is recommended
- Motion sickness was significant; recommend the student trial 2–3 short ground familiarisation sessions before committing; note that most students adapt
- Composure was substantially impaired by anxiety; recommend the student reflect and return for a second TIF or a ground session before committing

#text(size:12pt)[*Not recommended for training — reasons to be noted specifically*]
- English language or cognitive skills were, in the instructor's opinion, insufficient for safe flight training to be conducted (FOM S2.06(c) not satisfied)
- An obvious physical or cognitive impairment was observed that would prevent safe training
- The student disclosed a medical history or medication that warrants medical review before training commences — refer to the health standard requirements in FOM S2.16 and advise the student to seek appropriate medical advice before enrolling

#text(size:12pt)[*Under-18 — additional requirement*]
- If written parental/guardian consent was not provided and the flight proceeded, this must be recorded as a non-compliance in the instructor's record; the student may not continue training until consent is obtained
- *The TIF should not proceed without written consent for under-18 students*

- *Outcome*: TIF outcome recorded in the GCSFT booking/CRM system. If recommending training, brief Lesson 0 expectations. If not recommending, provide a written record for the student file.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Horizon clearly visible; ceiling >2000 ft AGL; surface wind \<20 knots; no convective activity; smooth air strongly preferred — turbulence significantly increases the risk of motion sickness and anxiety for a first-time passenger/student
- *FOM compliance*: All Section 2.06 pre-commencement requirements must be confirmed before the flight proceeds; the flight does not take off if any requirement cannot be met
- *Control transfers*: Handover procedure established before the first transfer; instructor retains full authority at all times; no control is transferred during takeoff, circuit, or landing phases
- *Motion sickness*: If symptoms appear during the flight, reduce workload, return to straight and level, and return to the aerodrome; do not continue training area exercises; inform the student that this is common and temporary
- *TEM*:
  - First-time passengers/students are unpredictable; monitor for unexpected control inputs (grabbing, squeezing, freezing); be prepared to take control at any moment
  - Brief the student before takeoff: "If you ever feel uncomfortable or want to stop, say so immediately; I will take control and we will return to the aerodrome"
  - Monitor the flight environment continuously; do not become so focused on the student that SA of the aircraft is lost
]

,homework:[
*There is no mandatory homework for the TIF. If the student chooses to enrol, the following is provided at debrief:*

- GCSFT Student Information Package (provided by reception)
- Recommended: Obtain Bob Tait's RAAus Study Guide before Lesson 0
- Recommended: Visit the NAIPS website (naips.airservicesaustralia.com) and create an account before Lesson 0
- Recommended: Read the GCSFT training pathway overview document
- If the student would like to read ahead before Lesson 0:
  - Bob Tait: Pg 101–107 (primary and further effects of controls)
  - Bob Tait: Pg 3 (IMSAFE)
- Next booking: Lesson 0 (ground only) is the first formal lesson; book at reception or online
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
